# {{repo}}

A Sutra-OS **Source Repository**: the transforms, functions and applications that
feed and surround the ontology. git carries the PYTHON (`transforms/*.py`);
`{{repo}}.ent`, `.enta` and `.entp` change through the CLI
(`sutra projection pull code-repository …` → `push --preview` → `push`), never
through `git push` — see `AGENTS.md` and `AUTHORING.md` in the ontology checkout.

This README is a crash course. It is seeded once and then yours — edit it.

---

## 1. Two planes

- **Entity plane** — `{{repo}}.ent`, `transforms/**`, `exports/**`.
  The platform INTERPRETS these; editing them edits the ontology.
- **File plane** — this README, `.gitignore`, `sutra.repo.edn`, `tests/**`,
  anything else. Stored and served verbatim; the platform does not read it.

## 2. What a push does

The git branch is the Sutra branch of the same name, and a push needs its open
proposal FIRST: `sutra branch B` → commit → `sutra propose "…" --source B
--target main` → `git pull --rebase` (the server commits declarations itself) →
`git push`. Without the proposal the push says `no open changeset — NOTHING was
written`. A `.py` no declaration names is removed by the server: push the file
and its declaration together. Never chain `merge-proposal` to a push that may
fail — an empty merge closes the branch.

Your commit is adopted into git FIRST, then checked: `scope` · `retire` ·
`lint` · `plan` · `test` · `ci/sutra-publish`. A commit that fails a check STAYS
in git and simply has not taken effect.

`retire` works here exactly as in the ontology repository, for this
repository's OWN members (those whose `:repository` is this one) — a member of
another repository is never touched. Leave a transform, function, pipeline or app
out of this tree and the push removes it; to be allowed, it must not be
`:active` (give it `:status :deprecated` with a `:deprecation {:reason …
:deadline … :replacement …}` or `:status :experimental` in an earlier commit —
unmarked counts as `:experimental`) and nothing that stays may still name it.
`projection push <text> --preview` shows the removals before you push.

The push prints the verdict, but that copy is perishable — a timeout takes it
with it, and a second push only says `Everything up-to-date`. Read it back with
`sutra checks [rev]`.

