## 3. The layer rule — read this one twice

    source → raw dataset → clean dataset → SHAPING hop → object type

A transform that binds a source straight to an object type lands raw rows in the
ontology with no curated layer in between. Split it:

1. an **extract** — expensive, possibly `:python`, possibly a model or egress;
   it lands a `:dataset`;
2. a **shaping hop** — cheap, deterministic, `:sql`, no egress, no model; it
   reads that dataset and writes the object type.

The payoff is asymmetric and that is the point: when the ontology's shape
changes you re-run only the cheap half. Bind the extract to the type directly
and every reshape re-pays the extraction.

The shaping hop is also where ETL columns STOP. `_ingested_at`, batch ids and
raw un-normalised strings stay in the dataset — still there for debugging, just
not selected into the type.

---

## 4. The three producer shapes

Exactly ONE of these per transform (`declare/valid-schema?` enforces the mutex —
pairing two is a validation error, not a widening):

| shape | writes | use it for |
|---|---|---|
| `:output` / `:outputs` | an object type, a link type or a dataset | the normal case; `:outputs` for a multi-output run |
| `:output-columns` | its own `t_<name>` table | an INTERMEDIATE: a chain link nobody needs to inspect on its own |

Prefer a `:dataset` output over an intermediate when you want to build, inspect
or rebuild the step by itself (`sutra build run --only <name>`).

`:primary-key` is not decoration. On an object-type output it is the identity
(§6). On an intermediate it is the incremental merge key — REQUIRED when
`:incremental` is set and it must be absent when it is not.

A transform naming a `:dataset` output must have that dataset DECLARED in the
ontology. Only an unnamed producer-minted one is implicit.

---

## 5. Which language

| language | reach for it when | why |
|---|---|---|
| `:sql` | projections, joins, aggregations over landed data | never leaves the engine; free to re-run |
| `:python` | extraction, parsing, models, anything needing egress | the sandbox subprocess, with the runtime façade |
| `:clojure` | small in-process shaping | runs in-process (SCI), no subprocess |

Say WHY in `:rationale` — it is queryable, and *python (not sql): the PDF
extraction needs the multimodal model* is the sentence the next person needs.

